      heading: "What is the \"rule of decades\" for ED?",
      body: `<p><strong>The "rule of decades" is a rough clinical shorthand from the Massachusetts Male Aging Study: the approximate percentage of men with ED loosely corresponds to their age by decade - so around 40% of men in their 40s, 50% in their 50s, 60% in their 60s, and 70% in their 70s have some degree of ED.</strong> It's a memory aid, not a precise law, but it captures the core trend well.</p><p>How to use it sensibly:</p><ul><li>It's a <strong>loose approximation</strong>, not an exact measurement - real study numbers vary around it, and the definition of "ED" moves the figures.</li><li>It describes <strong>"some degree" of ED</strong> (including mild cases), not severe ED, which is less common at every age.</li><li>Its real value is <strong>normalizing the experience</strong>: if roughly half the men your age (in your 50s) have some ED, it reframes the condition from "something is uniquely wrong with me" to "this is a common, expected, treatable part of male health."</li></ul><p>That reframing matters more than the exact math. A huge barrier to men getting help for ED is the false belief that it's rare or shameful. The rule of decades - imperfect as it is - makes the point that ED is statistically ordinary, especially with age. The right response to "lots of men my age have this" isn't resignation; it's the opposite - because the same decades of research that show how common ED is also show how treatable it is. Our <a href="/articles/best-ed-treatment">best ED treatment guide</a> covers the options.</p>`,
